# Everything here is produced by `make` from something that IS tracked.
# Nothing in this file is a source of truth; a fresh clone plus `make` recreates
# all of it. Anything tracked that the Makefile also writes would drift.

# Object files and the linked binary
build/
*.o
*.dSYM/

# The application bundle. Only the sources it is assembled from are tracked:
# Resources/Info.plist, Resources/TB_*.pdf and the icon.
Postview.app/

# The double-clickable harnesses, generated from Tools/*.sh by `make`.
Postview-Benchmark.command
Postview-Profile.command
Postview-Showdown.command

# Distributables
#
# Postview.zip is deliberately NOT ignored and IS committed. The machines
# this ships to run Mavericks and cannot build it -- the Makefile needs a
# modern clang for -Werror=unguarded-availability, which Xcode 6 lacks -- so
# a `git clone` has to be sufficient on its own to get a working app onto the
# target. Rebuild it with `make dist` and commit the result when the app
# changes.
dist/

# Benchmark and profile output. A showdown or profile run drops one of these
# wherever it was run from, and they are not history.
*.tsv
Benchmarks/

# ...with the exception of the ones that ARE history. Runs taken on the
# Mavericks machine are the evidence ENGINEERING.md argues from -- §9 quotes
# this file by name -- and a document that cites data the repository excludes
# is a document nobody can check. Same reasoning as Postview.zip above: a clone
# has to be sufficient on its own.
!Tests/Mavericks Testing/
!Tests/Mavericks Testing/*.tsv

# macOS
.DS_Store
